    heading: "Tennessee River Valley Climate — What It Means for Leighton Homes",
    body: `
      <p style="margin-bottom:20px;">Leighton's position along the Tennessee River places it in the highest-humidity part of the Shoals microclimate. The open water of Wilson Lake and the river corridor contribute to elevated atmospheric moisture that exceeds even the North Alabama average — and that moisture drives mold growth, allergen accumulation, and the slow deterioration of materials in homes that aren't actively managed for moisture. We address these consequences in Leighton homes every month.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom:20px;">The Tennessee River's flood potential is real in communities like Leighton that sit near the water. TVA's dam system manages most routine flood risk, but extreme events — the kind that overwhelm even managed systems — have affected the river corridor communities multiple times in living memory. Homeowners in Leighton who are within half a mile of the river or its tributaries carry genuine flood risk that standard homeowner's insurance may not cover without a separate flood policy. When these events occur, BamaClean responds.</p>
